r←{X} ∆WN Y;⎕TRAP;caller;z;t;seq
caller←findCaller

:If Y∧.=' '
    →0 ⊣ r←⍬
:EndIf

:If 2=⎕NC 'Y'
:AndIf (9=caller.⎕NC Y)∨(⊂Y)∊,¨'#' '⎕SE'
    Y←caller.⍎Y
:EndIf

⍝ (⊢Y). rather than Y. is load-bearing, not decoration, and it has to stay
⍝ INLINE at every use -- binding it to a name first (ref←⊢Y ⋄ ref.⎕NL) puts the
⍝ dotted form back and the problem returns.
⍝
⍝ In DIRECT mode ∆WC gives every GUI object reference semantics via 2(700⌶),
⍝ and a dotted access straight off such a reference is intercepted by the proxy
⍝ and dies in proxySpace exec with SYNTAX ERROR. Parenthesising defeats the
⍝ dotted-name parse and leaves an ordinary reference to dot into. Without it
⍝ ∆WN cannot see the children of anything ∆WC created -- which is every object
⍝ ⎕WN is ever asked about.

:If 0≠≢r←(⊢Y).⎕NL -9
:AndIf 0≠≢r←(2=⌊(⊢Y).⎕NC r,¨⊂'.Type')/r
    ⍝ ⎕NL sorts alphabetically, but ⎕WN reports children in CREATION order and
    ⍝ applications depend on that: pairing ⎕WN with ⎕WG'Points' to map drawn
    ⍝ children back to screen positions is only correct if the two agree. Object
    ⍝ names frequently encode something other than position -- a card game names
    ⍝ each card after the card, not its place in the pile -- so sorting silently
    ⍝ permutes the mapping and hit-tests pick the wrong object, with no error.
    ⍝
    ⍝ ∆WC keeps the order in EWC's own WCORDER rather than as a property on each
    ⍝ object, precisely so this lookup needs no dotting into a proxied namespace.
    ⍝ Names absent from the register (created some other way) sort last and keep
    ⍝ their relative order, since ⍋ is stable.
    seq←WCORDER⍳((⍕Y),'.')∘,¨r
    r←r[⍋seq]
    :If 2=⎕NC 'X'
        t←,⊆(⊢Y).⍎⍕r,¨⊂'.Type'
        r←(t∊⊆X)/r
    :EndIf
:EndIf
r←((⍕Y),'.')∘,¨r
